(6690246, 995766, 137689, '<td valign="top">Thanks all for your comments. It was the first time I had ever photographed kids and they don''t sit still for long. So I just clicked away as they fun playing on set, hence the strange composition and all other things that are wrong with the image (of which there are plenty). Through this challenge I have learnt that we can view the same picture but have it evoke completely contrasting emotions etc. When I look at her I see a beautiful little girl who has stunning personality. The tilted head is a pure "so what moment", something I have seen my nephews do over the years. <br/> <br/>I chose this image more for the sentimental value than its photographic credentials.<br/> <br/>Regards <br/>Sammy</td>', 'Thanks all for your comments. It was the first time I had ever photographed kids and they don''t sit still for long. So I just clicked away as they fun playing on set, hence the strange composition and all other things that are wrong with the image (of which there are plenty). Through this challenge I have learnt that we can view the same picture but have it evoke completely contrasting emotions etc. When I look at her I see a beautiful little girl who has stunning personality. The tilted head is a pure "so what moment", something I have seen my nephews do over the years.  I chose this image more for the sentimental value than its photographic credentials. Regards Sammy', '2012-02-24 07:55:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(6690250, 996148, 109387, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by tome:</b></div><hr/><i> Well I was sure this was Italy :). Just reminded me of some of your similar dramatic landscapes. Amazing shot, congrats on the top 10! </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thank you so much  :)<br/> <br/>Quite excited to hear that you recognized my style, this is great!<br/> <br/>But this is not Italy, I was in Norway this summer  ^_-<br/> <br/>But I have to say that Stelvio Pass could be quite similar, even if not so dramatic.<br/> <br/>ETA: Mark thank you for the Swisspass Rolerman video, it''s amazing!</td>', 'Originally posted by tome: Well I was sure this was Italy :). Just reminded me of some of your similar dramatic landscapes. Amazing shot, congrats on the top 10!   Thank you so much  :) Quite excited to hear that you recognized my style, this is great! But this is not Italy, I was in Norway this summer  ^_- But I have to say that Stelvio Pass could be quite similar, even if not so dramatic. ETA: Mark thank you for the Swisspass Rolerman video, it''s amazing!', '2012-02-24 08:01:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(6690251, 993597, 113591, '<td valign="top">I want it to be sort of uplifting, but it ends up disturbing my mind. So.. Well done, and thanks for another engaging image.</td>', 'I want it to be sort of uplifting, but it ends up disturbing my mind. So.. Well done, and thanks for another engaging image.', '2012-02-24 08:02:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(6690253, 993733, 68956, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by LalliSig:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Chinarosepetal:</b></div><hr/><i> Great lighting and backdrop, but once your brain has seen a girl with an amputated arm it refuses to see it any other way! I think I''d have preferred the model angled round so I could see the complete arm across her breasts. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>No.  Thx for the observation, but I do not agree with you.  Because of the scene behind her, the surroudings, this was by far the best angle to shoot from, and had she turned, like you suggested, because of the direction of the wind, the chiffon would not be blowing the way it is, her left hand is holding the other end of the material.  The only option to get that angle on her, would be for me to move further to the left, and then the clouds would not have aligned like they do, making for a far less interesting photo.<br/> <br/>Seeing how common it is to not see entire limbs in people photographs I am surprised to hear that your imagination works that way, had both of her hands been flat down her side of her body, would you have assumed her right arm was missing? </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I meant angle her upper body towards you (other elements staying the same)so she looks less like a paralympic athlete :) (not that there''s anything wrong with looking like a PA!)</td>', 'Originally posted by LalliSig: Originally posted by Chinarosepetal: Great lighting and backdrop, but once your brain has seen a girl with an amputated arm it refuses to see it any other way! I think I''d have preferred the model angled round so I could see the complete arm across her breasts.   No.  Thx for the observation, but I do not agree with you.  Because of the scene behind her, the surroudings, this was by far the best angle to shoot from, and had she turned, like you suggested, because of the direction of the wind, the chiffon would not be blowing the way it is, her left hand is holding the other end of the material.  The only option to get that angle on her, would be for me to move further to the left, and then the clouds would not have aligned like they do, making for a far less interesting photo. Seeing how common it is to not see entire limbs in people photographs I am surprised to hear that your imagination works that way, had both of her hands been flat down her side of her body, would you have assumed her right arm was missing?   I meant angle her upper body towards you (other elements staying the same)so she looks less like a paralympic athlete :) (not that there''s anything wrong with looking like a PA!)', '2012-02-24 08:05:17.000000', NULL, 0),
